 "description": "The Windows release a build number names (cveFeed.windows_builds). An NTLM challenge (rdp-ntlm-info, ms-sql-ntlm-info, http-ntlm-info, smtp-ntlm-info, imap-ntlm-info, pop3-ntlm-info, telnet-ntlm-info, nntp-ntlm-info) and smb-os-discovery state the major, minor and build of the system (10.0.20348): the release, never its update revision (UBR, the .2762 of 10.0.20348.2762), so the patch level is not known from outside. A server and a client release often share one build (10.0.17763 is Windows Server 2019 and Windows 10 Version 1809): each is listed as its own entry, and both are kept when nothing tells them apart. 'name' is the product name Microsoft's own CVE records use (the MSRC CNA, as in the CVE List); 'names' adds the other spellings those records and NVD's CPE products use for the same release (a '(Server Core installation)' suffix and a 'for x64-based Systems' platform are ignored when names are compared). 'end_of_support' is the last day Microsoft ships security updates to any edition of the release without a paid Extended Security Update (ESU) subscription (a Long-Term Servicing edition counts: it is why Microsoft's records keep naming the release); 'esu_until' the last day of the paid ESU programme, when there is one. Every date is read from the lifecycle page in 'lifecycle'; the build numbers from Microsoft's release information pages in 'sources'.",
